ASSIGNMENT 5 – SIGNAL HANDLING
============================================================

LAB ASSIGNMENT 1
------------------------------------------------------------
Write a C program which creates a child process which catches a signal
SIGHUP, SIGINT and SIGQUIT. The parent process sends a SIGHUP or SIGINT
signal after every 3 seconds. At the end of 30 seconds parent sends
SIGQUIT signal to child and child terminates by displaying:

"My DADDY has Killed me!!!"

PROGRAM:
------------------------------------------------------------

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>

void handle_signal(int sig)
{
    if (sig == SIGHUP)
    {
        printf("Child: SIGHUP received.\n");
    }
    else if (sig == SIGINT)
    {
        printf("Child: SIGINT received.\n");
    }
    else if (sig == SIGQUIT)
    {
        printf("My DADDY has Killed me!!!\n");
        exit(0);
    }
}

int main()
{
    pid_t pid;

    pid = fork();

    if (pid < 0)
    {
        perror("fork");
        return 1;
    }

    if (pid == 0)
    {
        signal(SIGHUP, handle_signal);
        signal(SIGINT, handle_signal);
        signal(SIGQUIT, handle_signal);

        printf("Child process started. PID = %d\n", getpid());

        while (1)
        {
            pause();
        }
    }
    else
    {
        int elapsed = 0;

        printf("Parent process started. Child PID = %d\n", pid);

        while (elapsed < 30)
        {
            sleep(3);
            elapsed += 3;

            if (elapsed < 30)
            {
                if ((elapsed / 3) % 2 == 1)
                {
                    printf("Parent: Sending SIGHUP to child.\n");
                    kill(pid, SIGHUP);
                }
                else
                {
                    printf("Parent: Sending SIGINT to child.\n");
                    kill(pid, SIGINT);
                }
            }
        }

        printf("Parent: 30 seconds completed. Sending SIGQUIT.\n");
        kill(pid, SIGQUIT);

        wait(NULL);

        printf("Parent: Child process terminated.\n");
    }

    return 0;
}

EXPLANATION:
- fork() creates the child process.
- signal() associates a signal handler with SIGHUP, SIGINT and SIGQUIT.
- The parent uses sleep(3) to wait for 3 seconds.
- kill() sends the required signal to the child.
- The child uses pause() to wait for a signal.
- After 30 seconds, the parent sends SIGQUIT.
- The SIGQUIT handler displays the required message and terminates
  the child.


LAB ASSIGNMENT 2
------------------------------------------------------------
Write a C program that illustrates suspending and resuming processes
using signals.

PROGRAM:
------------------------------------------------------------

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>

int main()
{
    pid_t pid;

    pid = fork();

    if (pid < 0)
    {
        perror("fork");
        return 1;
    }

    if (pid == 0)
    {
        while (1)
        {
            printf("Child process is running...\n");
            sleep(2);
        }
    }
    else
    {
        sleep(5);

        printf("Parent: Suspending child process.\n");
        kill(pid, SIGSTOP);

        sleep(5);

        printf("Parent: Resuming child process.\n");
        kill(pid, SIGCONT);

        sleep(5);

        printf("Parent: Terminating child process.\n");
        kill(pid, SIGTERM);

        wait(NULL);
        printf("Parent: Child process terminated.\n");
    }

    return 0;
}

EXPLANATION:
- SIGSTOP suspends the child process.
- While suspended, the child does not execute its normal instructions.
- SIGCONT resumes a stopped process.
- SIGTERM requests termination of the child.
- kill() is used by the parent to send these signals.


EXTRA PRACTICE QUESTION 3
------------------------------------------------------------
Write a C program handling both SIGINT (triggered by pressing Ctrl+C)
and SIGTERM (which can be sent to the process using the kill command)
using signal handlers.

PROGRAM:
------------------------------------------------------------

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <signal.h>

void handle_signal(int sig)
{
    if (sig == SIGINT)
    {
        printf("\nSIGINT received. Ctrl+C was pressed.\n");
    }
    else if (sig == SIGTERM)
    {
        printf("\nSIGTERM received. Process is terminating.\n");
        exit(0);
    }
}

int main()
{
    signal(SIGINT, handle_signal);
    signal(SIGTERM, handle_signal);

    printf("Process started. PID = %d\n", getpid());
    printf("Press Ctrl+C to generate SIGINT.\n");
    printf("Use 'kill -TERM %d' from another terminal to generate SIGTERM.\n",
           getpid());

    while (1)
    {
        printf("Process is running...\n");
        sleep(2);
    }

    return 0;
}

EXPLANATION:
- signal() registers the signal handler.
- Pressing Ctrl+C normally generates SIGINT.
- SIGTERM can be sent using the kill command.
- SIGINT is handled without terminating the process in this program.
- SIGTERM displays a message and terminates the process.


============================================================
IMPORTANT SYSTEM CALLS / FUNCTIONS USED
============================================================

1. malloc()
   Dynamically allocates a block of memory.

2. free()
   Releases dynamically allocated memory.

3. shmget()
   Creates or obtains a System V shared memory segment.

4. shmat()
   Attaches a shared memory segment to the process address space.

5. shmdt()
   Detaches shared memory from the process.

6. shmctl()
   Performs control operations on shared memory, including removal.

7. fork()
   Creates a new child process.

8. signal()
   Registers a function to handle a signal.

9. kill()
   Sends a signal to a process.

10. pause()
    Suspends the calling process until a signal is received.

11. sleep()
    Suspends execution for a specified number of seconds.

12. wait()
    Makes the parent wait for a child process to finish.

13. exit()
    Terminates the current process.

14. getpid()
    Returns the process ID of the calling process.


============================================================
SIGNALS USED
============================================================

SIGHUP
    Hangup signal.

SIGINT
    Interrupt signal, commonly generated using Ctrl+C.

SIGQUIT
    Quit signal.

SIGSTOP
    Stops/suspends a process.

SIGCONT
    Continues/resumes a stopped process.

SIGTERM
    Requests normal process termination.


